The user asks for block 2 only. `md replace` writes EVERY block back through
the formatter, thus block 1 and block 3 go through it too.

If they lose their backslashes, block 1 becomes a heading and block 3 becomes a
list. The user never asked for that, and the file now holds two blocks that mean
something else. Worse, block 3 is no longer block 3, thus the next command the
user runs writes over the wrong block.

Damage from an edit the user did not ask for is the worst shape this bug takes.
